# Call, Cast, and Await

This tutorial sends Signals to a running order Agent in each request mode, then shows what each mode tells the caller and what it leaves running.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Start an order Agent

`:pack` updates state at once. `:slow_pack` holds its Turn until this notebook sends it a message, which lets the later cells observe a Turn in progress. `:confirm` also answers its caller with a reply Signal.

```elixir
defmodule CallGuide.SlowPack do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "call_guide_slow_pack",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{order_id: Jido.Joi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, %{agent_state: state, notify: notify}) do
    send(notify, {:packing, self()})

    receive do
      :finish -> {:ok, %{state | packed: state.packed ++ [order_id]}}
    end
  end
end

defmodule CallGuide.OrderDesk do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_desk"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{packed: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/orders/desk"

    route "order.pack", as: :pack do
      action %{order_id: order_id},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{order_id: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | packed: context.agent_state.packed ++ [order_id]}}
      end
    end

    route "order.slow_pack", CallGuide.SlowPack, as: :slow_pack

    route "order.confirm", as: :confirm do
      action %{order_id: order_id},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{order_id: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, reply} = Jido.Agent.build_signal(context, "order.confirmed", %{order_id: order_id})
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [Jido.Agent.Directive.reply(reply)]}
      end
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: CallGuide.Jido)
{:ok, desk} = Jido.start_agent(CallGuide.Jido, CallGuide.OrderDesk, id: "desk-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(desk)
```

The server runs one Turn at a time. Every request mode below enters the same queue and passes the same commit step.

## Call and wait for the result

`Jido.AgentServer.call/3` waits for the commit and returns the committed Agent with the Turn's Directives.

```elixir
{:ok, agent, []} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-1"}))

agent.state
# The cell returns %{packed: ["o-1"]}
```

A failed Turn returns `{:error, reason}` and leaves the committed state as it was. A Signal with no matching route is one such failure.

```elixir
unknown = Jido.Signal.new!("order.teleport", %{}, source: "/orders/api")
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{code: :unmatched_input}} = Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, unknown)

Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(desk).agent.state
# The cell returns %{packed: ["o-1"]}
```

## Pass caller context

The `:context` option adds values to the Action context for one Turn. Jido does not store them in Agent state or in the Signal. `:turn_id`, `:trace_context`, and the Agent command keys such as `:agent_state` are reserved. The next cells use `:notify` to tell the Action which process to message.

## A caller timeout does not cancel the Turn

The third argument is a timeout in milliseconds or a keyword list. When the wait expires, the caller exits as with any OTP call. The Turn keeps running.

```elixir
slow = CallGuide.OrderDesk.slow_pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-2"})

outcome =
  try do
    Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, slow, timeout: 20, context: %{notify: self()})
  catch
    :exit, {:timeout, _details} -> :caller_stopped_waiting
  end

action =
  receive do
    {:packing, pid} -> pid
  end

active_stage = Jido.AgentServer.status(desk).active.stage
send(action, :finish)

{outcome, active_stage}
# The cell returns {:caller_stopped_waiting, :running}
```

The Action finished after the caller gave up, and its Turn committed. A later `call` runs after that Turn, so it sees `o-2`.

```elixir
{:ok, agent, []} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-3"}))

agent.state.packed
# The cell returns ["o-1", "o-2", "o-3"]
```

Use `Jido.AgentServer.cancel/2` to stop a Turn. See [Cancel Turns](cancel-turns.livemd).

## Cast without a reply

`Jido.AgentServer.cast/3` returns `:ok` after it sends the Signal. That `:ok` says nothing about the Turn. A cast that fails, or one dropped because the queue is full, only produces a log entry.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-4"}))
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(desk, unknown)

# A later call runs after both casts.
{:ok, agent, []} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-5"}))

{agent.state.packed, Jido.AgentServer.status(desk).agent_revision}
# The cell returns {["o-1", "o-2", "o-3", "o-4", "o-5"], 5}
```

The version counts committed Turns only. The two Signals with no route did not advance it.

## Submit now and await later

`Jido.AgentServer.submit/3` returns a handle at once. `Jido.AgentServer.await/2` collects the same result that `call/3` would return. An `await` timeout returns `{:error, :timeout}` without exiting, and you can wait again.

```elixir
pending =
  Jido.AgentServer.submit(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.slow_pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-6"}),
    context: %{notify: self()}
  )

action =
  receive do
    {:packing, pid} -> pid
  end

first_wait = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending, 0)
send(action, :finish)
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.await(pending)

{first_wait, List.last(agent.state.packed)}
# The cell returns {{:error, :timeout}, "o-6"}
```

Submit several requests to run them in order while your process does other work. Each result shows the state after its own Turn.

```elixir
handles =
  for order_id <- ["o-7", "o-8"] do
    Jido.AgentServer.submit(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: order_id}))
  end

handles
|> Enum.map(&Jido.AgentServer.await/1)
|> Enum.map(fn {:ok, agent, []} -> List.last(agent.state.packed) end)
# The cell returns ["o-7", "o-8"]
```

A handle has these limits:

- Only the submitting process can await or abandon it. Another process gets a `Jido.Error.ValidationError`.
- You can collect a result once. Do not await a handle after you collect or abandon it.
- A handle does not survive a restart of your process.

Call `Jido.AgentServer.abandon/1` when you no longer want the reply. The Turn still runs.

```elixir
abandoned = Jido.AgentServer.submit(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-9"}))
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.abandon(abandoned)

{:ok, agent, []} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.pack_signal!(%{order_id: "o-10"}))

Enum.take(agent.state.packed, -2)
# The cell returns ["o-9", "o-10"]
```

If the server stops before it replies, `await/2` returns `{:error, %Jido.Error.ExecutionError{}}`.

## Receive a reply Signal

A reply is a separate Signal that answers the request. It reaches the calling process as a plain message after the Turn commits. The return value of `call/3` still holds the Agent and the Directives.

```elixir
{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{}]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(desk, CallGuide.OrderDesk.confirm_signal!(%{order_id: "o-10"}))

receive do
  %Jido.Signal{type: "order.confirmed", data: data} -> data
after
  1_000 -> :no_reply
end
# The cell returns %{order_id: "o-10"}
```

A cast has no caller to answer, so a cast that produces a reply fails before commit. See [Agent Turns and Replies](../agents/turns-state-and-replies.md).

## Read without a Turn

Reads answer while a Turn runs, and they do not join the Turn queue. They see committed data only.

| Function | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `Jido.AgentServer.agent/2` | The committed Agent. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.snapshot/2` | The committed Agent and its `state_version`, read in the same server event. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.plugin_state/3` | One committed Plugin-owned field. |
| `Jido.AgentServer.status/2` | A runtime view. See [Status and Turn History](status-and-history.md). |

```elixir
%{agent: %{id: "desk-1"}, state_version: version} = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(desk)
is_integer(version)
# The cell returns true
```

## Choose a request mode

| You need | Use | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The result before you continue | `Jido.AgentServer.call/3` | `{:ok, agent, directives}` or `{:error, reason}`. A timeout exits the caller. |
| The result later, from the same process | `Jido.AgentServer.submit/3`, then `Jido.AgentServer.await/2` | The same result. A timeout returns `{:error, :timeout}`. |
| No result | `Jido.AgentServer.cast/3` | `:ok` when the Signal is sent. Delivery is best effort. |

`Jido.call/4`, `Jido.submit/4`, and `Jido.cast/3` do the same through a `Jido.Agent.Ref`. `Jido.cast/3` has no `:context` option.

